# libxtc ThreadSanitizer suppressions -- known-benign runtime internals.
#
# These are NOT bugs.  Each entry is a specific, understood, documented
# intentional benign race, not a blanket silence.  The clang tsan-fibers
# CI job runs a curated fiber subset that does not include these paths;
# this file lets a FULL-suite TSan run (all m-suites) stay clean so real
# races -- like the exec.c loop_node race fixed with relaxed atomics --
# still surface loudly.
#
# Load with:  TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=.github/tsan.supp
#
# To extend: confirm the race is genuinely benign (side-effect-free read
# used only as a hint that another mechanism re-checks), then add a
# stanza with a comment explaining WHY.

# __exec_has_work() is the executor supervisor's quiescence poll (see the
# block comment at src/evt/exec.c).  It reads each loop's n_timers and
# inbox.head WITHOUT the owning worker's lock, on purpose: the reads are
# side-effect-free "is non-zero?" hints, and the supervisor double-checks
# after a short sleep before it stops the executor, which absorbs any
# stale read.  Making n_timers atomic would push atomic ops onto the
# timer hot path (loop->timers[loop->n_timers++], sift-up/down) purely to
# satisfy a poll -- not worth it.  The authoritative liveness signals it
# also consults (n_alive, the work-stealing deque) ARE atomic/lock-free.
race:__exec_has_work

# xtc_exec_run's inline idle re-check (src/evt/exec.c ~605) reads
# inbox.head for the same quiescence decision as __exec_has_work above,
# same rationale.
race:xtc_exec_run

# The work-stealing enqueue/steal handoff races on the stolen task's
# q_next scheduling-list pointer: a thief resets stolen->q_next before
# re-enqueuing it (src/evt/loop.c __xtc_loop_step_once) while the
# original owner's __xtc_loop_enqueue() reads t->q_next in its
# fast-path double-enqueue guard.  The CORRECTNESS arbiter is the
# Chase-Lev deque's seq_cst steal CAS (formally race-free for the deque
# slot); TSan cannot carry that happens-before to the pointed-to task's
# internal fields.  The q_next read is only a "already queued?" hint and
# a stale read is safe -- proven by test_steal's no_loss_no_double_run
# invariants (every task runs exactly once).  Making q_next atomic would
# push atomic ops onto every FIFO list operation for a benign hint.
race:__xtc_loop_enqueue
race:__xtc_loop_step_once
